1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,644
♪ [music] ♪

2
00:00:13,597 --> 00:00:16,570
- [Tyler] Previously, we discussed
asymmetric information

3
00:00:16,570 --> 00:00:19,723
and how that can cause
adverse selection in markets.

4
00:00:19,723 --> 00:00:22,652
In this video, we'll tackle
how asymmetric information

5
00:00:22,652 --> 00:00:24,946
can lead to moral hazard.

6
00:00:25,228 --> 00:00:27,159
Let's start with an example.

7
00:00:27,159 --> 00:00:29,490
Imagine you're buying
a bottle of water.

8
00:00:29,490 --> 00:00:31,073
You probably have
a pretty good idea

9
00:00:31,073 --> 00:00:34,454
of what you're getting, H2O,
especially if you've bought

10
00:00:34,454 --> 00:00:36,545
the same brand of water before.

11
00:00:36,546 --> 00:00:39,158
The information that the seller
and the buyer have

12
00:00:39,158 --> 00:00:41,045
is pretty close to equal.

13
00:00:41,411 --> 00:00:43,892
But things get more complicated
if you're dealing

14
00:00:43,893 --> 00:00:46,362
with something
like repairing your car.

15
00:00:46,892 --> 00:00:49,384
The mechanics says you need
a Johnson rod.

16
00:00:49,384 --> 00:00:52,685
Do you? Do you even know
what a Johnson rod is?

17
00:00:52,686 --> 00:00:55,685
The mechanic knows a lot more
about car repair than you do,

18
00:00:55,685 --> 00:00:58,790
and that makes it hard to know
whether the mechanic is correct

19
00:00:58,790 --> 00:01:00,479
or even telling the truth.

20
00:01:00,479 --> 00:01:03,428
You might end up paying
a lot of money for car repairs

21
00:01:03,428 --> 00:01:05,116
you don't really need.

22
00:01:05,117 --> 00:01:08,264
This is another example
of asymmetric information

23
00:01:08,264 --> 00:01:12,235
where one party to an exchange
has more or better information

24
00:01:12,235 --> 00:01:13,789
than the other party has.

25
00:01:14,334 --> 00:01:17,925
As we'll see, asymmetric
information can challenge markets

26
00:01:17,925 --> 00:01:20,074
and sometimes cause them to fail.

27
00:01:20,589 --> 00:01:24,188
When one party to an exchange
has an information advantage,

28
00:01:24,189 --> 00:01:26,811
they may have an incentive
to use that advantage

29
00:01:26,811 --> 00:01:30,426
to exploit the other party,
and that temptation to exploit

30
00:01:30,427 --> 00:01:32,525
is called moral hazard.

31
00:01:33,082 --> 00:01:35,548
The car repair problem
is just one example

32
00:01:35,548 --> 00:01:38,708
of asymmetric information
and moral hazard.

33
00:01:38,717 --> 00:01:41,691
A taxi driver has more information
about the roads

34
00:01:41,691 --> 00:01:43,213
than does a tourist.

35
00:01:43,213 --> 00:01:46,320
He may take a longer route
in order to get a higher fare.

36
00:01:47,089 --> 00:01:51,159
A restaurant owner knows more
about the safety of his restaurant

37
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:53,611
than does his insurance company.

38
00:01:53,611 --> 00:01:55,786
He might choose
to skimp on sprinklers

39
00:01:55,786 --> 00:01:57,975
if he's got fire insurance.

40
00:01:58,514 --> 00:02:01,589
An employee knows
what he's been working on all day

41
00:02:01,589 --> 00:02:04,075
whereas the manager
maybe does not.

42
00:02:04,075 --> 00:02:07,906
The employee might choose
to goof off rather than do his job.

43
00:02:08,321 --> 00:02:12,530
These examples highlight a concept
called the principal-agent problem.

44
00:02:12,884 --> 00:02:16,301
Often when you hire someone,
that person has more information

45
00:02:16,301 --> 00:02:17,411
than you do.

46
00:02:17,411 --> 00:02:20,157
Indeed, that's often why
you pay to hire them.

47
00:02:20,158 --> 00:02:22,971
In the case of your car,
you are the principal

48
00:02:22,971 --> 00:02:25,168
and the mechanic is your agent.

49
00:02:25,168 --> 00:02:27,359
Your incentive
is to get your car fixed

50
00:02:27,359 --> 00:02:29,601
and not waste too much money.

51
00:02:29,602 --> 00:02:32,020
His incentive might be
to get as much money

52
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:33,774
out of you as possible.

53
00:02:33,774 --> 00:02:36,989
Given that he has information
about cars that you don't,

54
00:02:36,989 --> 00:02:39,421
he can lie to charge more.

55
00:02:39,421 --> 00:02:42,086
In this case there
are conflicting incentives,

56
00:02:42,086 --> 00:02:44,168
and you don't have
the information to know

57
00:02:44,169 --> 00:02:46,205
a good deal from a bad deal.

58
00:02:46,824 --> 00:02:50,006
Ideally, you would like
to align the incentives

59
00:02:50,006 --> 00:02:53,101
of the mechanic with yours,
so you don't get swindled.

60
00:02:53,428 --> 00:02:55,812
That is at least, in principle,
how you can solve

61
00:02:55,813 --> 00:02:57,975
a principal-agent problem.

62
00:02:57,975 --> 00:03:01,654
No one likes to be ripped off,
but the problem of moral hazard

63
00:03:01,654 --> 00:03:04,152
runs deeper than just the rip-off.

64
00:03:04,152 --> 00:03:07,482
The bigger problem
is that the potential for a rip-off

65
00:03:07,482 --> 00:03:10,914
means that a transaction
maybe less likely to occur

66
00:03:10,914 --> 00:03:12,377
in the first place.

67
00:03:12,377 --> 00:03:15,078
If you know the mechanic
may recommend more service

68
00:03:15,078 --> 00:03:17,387
than is necessary, 
you might, for instance,

69
00:03:17,387 --> 00:03:20,650
pass on some recommended
precautionary repairs

70
00:03:20,650 --> 00:03:23,567
and just wait
until your car breaks down.

71
00:03:23,567 --> 00:03:25,683
Of course, that can be inefficient.

72
00:03:25,683 --> 00:03:28,274
You’d prefer to perform
that preventive maintenance

73
00:03:28,274 --> 00:03:30,746
and not break down unexpectedly.

74
00:03:30,746 --> 00:03:32,769
But because
of asymmetric information,

75
00:03:32,769 --> 00:03:35,209
you can't trust your agent,
the mechanic,

76
00:03:35,209 --> 00:03:38,982
and so you pass on those repairs
for fear of being ripped-off.

77
00:03:39,494 --> 00:03:43,252
So we can see that asymmetric
information can impede trade

78
00:03:43,252 --> 00:03:47,254
and limit the great benefits
of specialization through markets.

79
00:03:47,804 --> 00:03:49,420
So what can we do?

80
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:51,296
Well, take a moment
to think about this,

81
00:03:51,296 --> 00:03:54,202
and perhaps you can anticipate
some of the solutions

82
00:03:54,202 --> 00:03:56,625
we'll discuss in the next video.

